\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
Active sonar systems produce signal data that require organised processing
and interpretation. This project focuses on developing a Windows desktop
application for channel-wise ship-wake data processing and a separate
deep-learning workflow for predicting wake labels from sonar signal data.

The project contains two application stages. The first is a rule-based
signal-processing GUI developed using Python and PySide6. It accepts Beam PRI
data and corresponding PRI Analysis data, associates the Beam file with its
PRI information, applies the specified labelling rules, and generates a
channel-wise labelled output file. The processing workflow identifies the
required I-channel values and excludes Q-channel values where required by the
data specification.

The second stage concerns supervised deep-learning model development. Beam
signal data are associated with channel labels derived from the Wake\_Detection
information, subject to verification of the channel-to-bit mapping. In the
EfficientNet-based workflow, each channel signal is represented as a
time-frequency spectrogram. The spectrogram is prepared as an image input and
supplied to an EfficientNet-B0-based binary classification model implemented
with TensorFlow and Keras. The trained model is saved and can subsequently be
loaded by a prediction application to process new Beam data and produce
channel-wise YES/NO predictions.

The project brings together CSV file handling, PRI matching, signal
preprocessing, rule-based labelling, spectrogram generation, deep-learning
model training, model persistence, and desktop application deployment.
Model performance must be established using an independent test set containing
recordings not used during training; training performance alone is not treated
as evidence of generalisation.

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Active sonar, ship-wake detection, Beam PRI,
I/Q signal, signal processing, spectrogram, EfficientNet-B0, TensorFlow,
Keras, PySide6, supervised learning.

