\chapter{Introduction}
\section{Background}
Sonar is used to observe and analyse underwater acoustic information. Active
sonar systems transmit acoustic pulses and analyse received responses. The
resulting data may be stored in structured files and processed to identify
signal characteristics associated with a target or wake.

This project addresses the need for a convenient software workflow to process
Beam PRI data, associate it with PRI Analysis information, create channel-wise
labels using specified rules, and investigate a learned prediction approach
using machine learning.

\section{Problem Statement}
Processing sonar datasets manually can be time-consuming and can make
consistent file handling difficult. A software tool is required to accept the
relevant data files, associate records using PRI information, apply the
required labelling logic, and save organised outputs. In addition, a trained
model can be investigated as a complementary approach for predicting
channel-wise wake labels from signal patterns.

\section{Objectives}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item Develop a Windows desktop GUI for Beam and PRI Analysis file selection.
\item Match each Beam PRI file with its corresponding PRI record.
\item Extract the required I-channel signal values and handle Q-channel
columns according to the data format.
\item Apply the specified wake-labelling rules and generate labelled outputs.
\item Prepare channel-level signal examples and corresponding labels for
supervised learning.
\item Convert signal sequences into spectrogram representations for the
EfficientNet-based workflow.
\item Train, save, load, and use a deep-learning model for channel-wise
prediction.
\item Provide a desktop prediction workflow that can be packaged for
compatible Windows systems.
\end{itemize}

\section{Project Scope}
The project covers CSV-based data processing, PRI matching, channel
extraction, rule-based labelling, spectrogram preparation, EfficientNet-B0
model development, prediction, and Windows GUI deployment. The reliability
of the final prediction system depends on correct labels, consistent
preprocessing, suitable training data, and independent evaluation.

\chapter{Overview of the Proposed System}
\section{System Components}
The complete workflow is divided into three components:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Rule-based processing GUI:} It accepts Beam and PRI Analysis files
and generates labelled channel output.
\item \textbf{Training pipeline:} It prepares labelled signal examples, generates
spectrograms, trains EfficientNet-B0, and saves the model.
\item \textbf{AI prediction GUI:}It loads the saved model, preprocesses new Beam
data consistently, and displays or saves predicted labels.
\end{enumerate}

