\chapter{Dataset Preparation and Signal Preprocessing}
\section{Data Organisation}
The training data are organised into Beam PRI CSV files and the corresponding
PRI Analysis CSV file or files. The Beam data provide the signal input, while
the analysis information provides the supervisory labels used by the
training pipeline.

\section{I-Channel Extraction}
Where Beam columns are arranged as alternating I/Q pairs, the I-channel
columns are selected by their positions, while Q-channel columns are
excluded. Where reliable column names are available, the implementation may
use those names instead. The extraction logic must match the actual CSV
structure.

Special care is taken not to lose the first observation. For a headerless
CSV, the file must be read without treating the first numerical row as a
column header. Missing-value handling should remove only invalid entries and
must not remove a valid first sample.

\section{Label Preparation}
The Wake\_Detection field is converted into channel-wise binary targets
according to the verified label specification:
\[
\text{YES} \rightarrow 1,\qquad \text{NO} \rightarrow 0.
\]
Correct label generation is essential because supervised learning uses
these values as the expected targets.

\section{Spectrogram Generation}
A spectrogram represents how signal energy varies with time and frequency.
It is calculated from a channel signal using a short-time Fourier
transform-based method. The resulting power values can be converted to a
logarithmic scale and normalised before being resized to the model input
dimensions.

The same preprocessing steps and settings must be used during both training
and inference. Changes to signal scaling, spectrogram parameters, image
dimensions, or channel ordering can affect model predictions.

