\chapter{EfficientNet-Based Model Training}
\section{Supervised Learning Approach}
The model-training stage uses supervised learning. Each training example
consists of a preprocessed channel signal representation and its corresponding
known binary label. The model learns a mapping between spectrogram patterns
and the channel label.

\section{Why EfficientNet-B0}
EfficientNet-B0 is a convolutional neural network architecture designed to
extract features from image inputs with a balance between model capacity and
computational cost. Since signal preprocessing produces spectrogram images,
EfficientNet-B0 is used as the image feature-extraction backbone.

The model configuration must be described accurately. If the network is
created with \texttt{weights=None}, it is trained from randomly initialised
weights rather than ImageNet pretrained weights.

\section{Classification Head}
The original classification head of EfficientNet is omitted when it is used
as a feature extractor. A task-specific classification head is added. In the
binary channel-classification workflow, the head produces one sigmoid score
for a channel example:
\[
\sigma(z)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}.
\]
A decision threshold converts the score into a binary label. A threshold
such as 0.5 is a starting configuration and should be reviewed using
validation data.

\section{Loss Function and Optimiser}
Binary cross-entropy is suitable for a binary classification output. The
optimiser updates the trainable model parameters to reduce the loss during
training. Learning rate, batch size, number of epochs, and other
hyperparameters are configuration choices and should be documented from
the final training run.

\section{Training, Validation, and Testing}
The dataset should be divided into training, validation, and test groups.
When multiple PRI files originate from the same recording or acquisition,
related files should be kept in the same split where possible. This reduces
the risk of information leakage between training and evaluation data.

The validation set is used for model selection and threshold tuning. The
test set is reserved for final evaluation and should not be used to tune
the model. Reported performance should include precision, recall, F1-score,
and a confusion matrix in addition to accuracy.

\section{Class Imbalance}
If the number of YES and NO labels differs substantially, accuracy alone
may be misleading. Class distribution should be examined, and class
weighting or other suitable imbalance-handling methods may be considered.
Performance should be inspected separately for each channel and class.

\section{Saving the Trained Model}
After training and selection, the model is saved in Keras format, for
example \texttt{wake\_efficientnet.keras}. The saved model is loaded later
by the prediction application. The preprocessing configuration, input
dimensions, channel ordering, model version, and decision threshold should
also be retained so inference matches training.

