\chapter{AI-Based Wake Prediction GUI}
\section{Purpose}
The second GUI is intended for inference using the saved model. It does not
need to retrain the network whenever the application is opened. Instead,
it loads the saved model and applies the same preprocessing pipeline to
new Beam data.

\section{Prediction Workflow}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Select a new Beam PRI CSV file.
\item Select the associated PRI Analysis file if required by the
application's PRI validation workflow.
\item Select the destination for the prediction output.
\item Load the saved Keras model.
\item Extract I-channel signal values using the same rules applied during
training.
\item Generate spectrogram inputs using the same parameters and
normalisation settings as training.
\item Run model inference and obtain a score for each channel.
\item Apply the configured threshold to obtain YES/NO predictions.
\item Display and save the channel-wise prediction results.
\end{enumerate}

The prediction labels must be produced by the trained model. The
Wake\_Detection field should not determine the AI prediction itself. If the
Analysis file is selected in the prediction GUI, its role should be limited
to PRI matching or validation unless the implementation explicitly defines
another purpose.

\section{Prediction Output}
A prediction CSV may contain the channel identifier, model score, predicted
YES/NO label, and optional signal summary statistics. The output column
names should match the actual implementation.

